#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088778} :activity/id #uuid "10d02fcd-c546-5af1-b0ee-b92aa67beb52" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049037 :topic/id #uuid "d5569c8d-5b80-5e0c-9284-7d090bd4c69a" :topic/math-academy-id 808} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088778 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088939 :tutorial/content "A **horizontal asymptote** is a horizontal line that a graph of a function approaches as $x$ tends to (positive or negative) infinity.\n\nFor example, let's consider the rational function\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\frac{2x^2}{x^2-1}.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/ec/ec8071dc9280cf344126ec3d6d9287823fc81c3ade910c98191a1d4d43ec0d6e.png)\n\nWe can see that $y=f(x)$ has the horizontal asymptote $y=2.$ The function gets closer and closer to this line but never touches it.\n\nBut how can we calculate the horizontal asymptote of a function without using a graph?\n\nTo find the horizontal asymptote of a rational function, we follow these three steps.\n\n1\\. Identify the **dominant term** in the denominator. The dominant term is the leading term of the polynomial in the denominator, though we usually ignore the coefficient.\n\n2\\. Divide every term in the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$ by the dominant term.\n\n3\\. Evaluate the remaining expression as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nIn our case, the dominant term is $x^2.$ So, we divide every term in the numerator and denominator by $x^2,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}-1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\frac{2{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}}{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhen $x$ is very large, the $\\dfrac{1}{x^2}$term becomes very small. Therefore, as $x\\to\\infty,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(\\frac{2}{1-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}}) \\\\  & \\to \\frac{2}{1-0} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the horizontal asymptote is $y=2.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d23ef418-713b-5f13-8463-2cd30f9458e5" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3776 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "1492a6f8-d490-54b0-9792-5135334cf77f" :step/math-academy-id 467901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088779}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088779 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087851 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087578 :question/id #uuid "eb0b899e-af3a-587c-96ae-f1da9e0929dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-5847"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d9d8d4d2-fef7-5ada-8691-3f504cb3cf97" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049313}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088188} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294939} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294940} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186294941} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434786} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186434789} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186563698}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Horizontal Asymptote When the Degrees of the Numerator and Denominator are Equal"} :step/id #uuid "6e2fe124-312a-5cc5-b3f0-cf7d27eed5e9" :step/math-academy-id 467902 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088780}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088780 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087852 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087579 :question/id #uuid "0d53819b-ca24-549d-98e2-a9b78b49c080" :question/math-academy-id "e-5849"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6f75252e-bf7d-59ed-bb16-6a2b65a97863" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088279} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088280} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247327} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292691} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329774} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378415} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386647} {